{
  "$schema": "../../../../../../../../../../🧰️framework/🛍️products/🦑️repo/🔨️modules/🧪️test/🧬️schema/🔣️.json",
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "_comment": "🧩️ AVI 1.0/🎛️hdrl's own contribution to the repository test platform — its mutation oracle registration and its declared mutation vocabulary (wave 7, ticket 26/08/23/END-TO-END-TESTING-REFACTOR). No standalone Rust crate reads AND writes AVI credibly (`avirus` 0.2.5 never parses a typed header and its 'write' path copies the whole hdrl section byte-for-byte; `rff-format-avi` 0.1.0's own doc comment says its muxer is 'still scaffolded'; `oxideav-avi` 0.0.9 has real depth but zero independent adoption — created 2026-04-19, 0 GitHub stars/forks, would pull in an equally unvalidated `oxideav-core`). AVI IS a RIFF container, so the reference here composes `riff` 2.0 — a mature, independent, heavily used (11.9M downloads) generic RIFF/LIST/chunk reader/writer, MIT, unrelated to this repository — with a hand-written AVI 1.0 hdrl/strl/movi/idx1 codec against the format's own public byte layout. Same composition shape ✏️s/🔌️plugins/🗄️stdio/🗿️artifacts/💬️bcf's oracle already established for zip+quick-xml, for the identical reason: no standalone crate exists for the specific format, so two independent, credible, more-generic layers are composed instead of adopting a weaker/unlicensed substitute. Full investigation and evidence: .🧬semio/🦑️repo/🎫️tickets/🎆️26/🌙️08/☀️23/END-TO-END-TESTING-REFACTOR/📓️w7-avi-1-0-mutate-report.md. Discovered by convention alongside ✏️s/🔌️plugins/🗄️stdio's own top-level manifest; merged into one registry, no central edit required.",
  "oracles": [
    {
      "id": "riff-avi-1-0-mutate",
      "ecosystem": "rust",
      "package": "riff",
      "version": "2.0",
      "capabilities": [
        "avi-1-0-mutate-second-implementation"
